::ENG4_U01_L01_GP_Q1::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: Careful reading is not the same as slow reading. A careful reader notices how each sentence changes or refines the idea before it. When a writer qualifies a claim with words such as however, although, or specifically, the reader must preserve that qualification in a paraphrase. A strong paraphrase does more than replace words with synonyms; it restates the full meaning, including relationships among ideas, limits on claims, and the author's emphasis. In college and workplace settings, inaccurate paraphrase can distort a source, weaken an argument, or create confusion about what a document actually says. Which paraphrase best preserves the first sentence? {
~A. Careful reading always requires students to read slowly.#This changes the meaning by turning a contrast into a rule about speed.
=B. Careful reading involves attention to meaning rather than simply reading at a slower pace.#Correct. This preserves the contrast between careful reading and slow reading.
~C. Slow reading is the best way to understand all academic texts.#This adds a claim the passage does not make.
~D. Careful reading and slow reading are basically identical.#This contradicts the passage.
}
::ENG4_U01_L01_GP_Q2::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: Careful reading is not the same as slow reading. A careful reader notices how each sentence changes or refines the idea before it. When a writer qualifies a claim with words such as however, although, or specifically, the reader must preserve that qualification in a paraphrase. A strong paraphrase does more than replace words with synonyms; it restates the full meaning, including relationships among ideas, limits on claims, and the author's emphasis. In college and workplace settings, inaccurate paraphrase can distort a source, weaken an argument, or create confusion about what a document actually says. What must a paraphrase preserve when a writer uses words such as however or although? {
~A. The exact sentence length of the original#Sentence length does not determine whether meaning is preserved.
~B. Only the vocabulary words that sound academic#Academic vocabulary matters, but the question asks about qualification.
~C. The writer's personal background#The passage does not discuss the writer's background.
=D. The qualification or limit placed on the claim#Correct. Words such as however and although often narrow or qualify claims.
}
::ENG4_U01_L01_GP_Q3::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: Careful reading is not the same as slow reading. A careful reader notices how each sentence changes or refines the idea before it. When a writer qualifies a claim with words such as however, although, or specifically, the reader must preserve that qualification in a paraphrase. A strong paraphrase does more than replace words with synonyms; it restates the full meaning, including relationships among ideas, limits on claims, and the author's emphasis. In college and workplace settings, inaccurate paraphrase can distort a source, weaken an argument, or create confusion about what a document actually says. In this passage, what does distort most nearly mean? {
=A. Change in a way that misrepresents meaning#Correct. The passage warns that inaccurate paraphrase can misrepresent a source.
~B. Make a passage shorter#Shortening is summarizing, not necessarily distorting.
~C. Read a document more slowly#The word refers to changed meaning, not reading speed.
~D. Quote a source exactly#Exact quotation is not distortion.
}
::ENG4_U01_L01_GP_Q4::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: Careful reading is not the same as slow reading. A careful reader notices how each sentence changes or refines the idea before it. When a writer qualifies a claim with words such as however, although, or specifically, the reader must preserve that qualification in a paraphrase. A strong paraphrase does more than replace words with synonyms; it restates the full meaning, including relationships among ideas, limits on claims, and the author's emphasis. Which statement best evaluates this paraphrase: A strong paraphrase changes the words in a source to synonyms. {
~A. It is accurate because synonym replacement is the main purpose of paraphrase.#This misses the passage's warning that paraphrase does more than replace words.
~B. It is accurate because it keeps every detail from the passage.#It does not keep every detail; it narrows the idea incorrectly.
=C. It is incomplete because it ignores full meaning, relationships, limits, and emphasis.#Correct. The paraphrase leaves out the standard for a strong paraphrase.
~D. It is effective because it is shorter than the original sentence.#Being shorter does not make a paraphrase accurate.
}
::ENG4_U01_L01_GP_Q5::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Passage: Careful reading is not the same as slow reading. A careful reader notices how each sentence changes or refines the idea before it. When a writer qualifies a claim with words such as however, although, or specifically, the reader must preserve that qualification in a paraphrase. A strong paraphrase does more than replace words with synonyms; it restates the full meaning, including relationships among ideas, limits on claims, and the author's emphasis. Which sentence uses standard English conventions to express an accurate paraphrase? {
~A. Careful reading are when you read slow and switch words.#This sentence has grammar errors and changes the meaning.
=B. Careful reading requires attention to how each sentence develops the writer's meaning.#Correct. The sentence is grammatically clear and accurate.
~C. The author say paraphrases is synonyms and nothing else.#This sentence has grammar errors and contradicts the passage.
~D. Slow reading, careful reading, and synonym replacing is the same thing.#This sentence has agreement errors and distorts the passage.
}
